abstract: >-
  A formal-methods study of governed computation: how evidence, authority,
  custody, spend, history, refusal, and obligation constrain which conclusions
  and state transitions are justified.

  V16 adds Governed Transition Boundaries, a public-evidence surface asking
  whether one total decoder recovers a selected target from a selected view of
  the source, correctly for every source. Four axiom-free results establish
  that such explicit factorizations compose, imply fibre constancy, are blocked
  by a target-distinguishing collision, and are not restored by deterministic
  postprocessing of an insufficient view. A declared seven-coordinate finite
  language carries a unique least target-determining selection, and five
  separately scoped witnesses cover authorization refusal, context validation,
  capacity realizability, occurrence-link observation, and hidden-relation
  nonidentifiability. The generic statements are standard
  function-factorization and view-determinacy facts and the finite result is an
  exhaustive dependency calculation; the contribution is their mechanically
  checked synthesis. No novelty or priority is claimed.

  V16 promotes no stable surface. The V15 Cross-Calculus Atlas remains
  authoritative for selected edges from Governed Transport, Execution Custody,
  and Continuity Admission, retaining their native judgment indices and exact
  semantic receipts; its ATLAS classification is unchanged.

  Lean checks these theorems under their disclosed definitions and axiom
  footprints; no runtime conformance is established. The complete non-claim
  ledger is in docs/V16-PUBLIC-INDEX.md.
